Q: Give “sunshine” in French with the article used after `On annonce`.
A: {{`Du soleil.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter a, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give “wind” in French with the article used after `On annonce`.
A: {{`Du vent.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter b, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give “snow” in French with the article used after `On prévoit`.
A: {{`De la neige.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter c, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give “rain” in French with the article used after `On annonce`.
A: {{`De la pluie.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter d, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give “clouds” in French with the article used in the forecast exercise.
A: {{`Des nuages.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter e, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a thunderstorm” in French.
A: {{`Un orage.`}}
N: CD2 piste 27, letter f, manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a rainbow” in French.
A: {{`Un arc-en-ciel.`}}
N: Retain the hyphens. Cahier p. 82. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “lightning flashes” in French, with a plural article.
A: {{`Des éclairs.`}}
N: The visible flashes; thunder is `du tonnerre`. Cahier p. 82. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give “thunder” in French with its partitive article.
A: {{`Du tonnerre.`}}
N: The sound accompanying a thunderstorm. Cahier p. 82. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is hot” about the weather, using `faire`.
A: {{`Il fait chaud.`}}
N: For a person's sensation, use `J'ai chaud`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is cold” about the weather, using `faire`.
A: {{`Il fait froid.`}}
N: The weather `il` is impersonal. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “The weather is nice” using `Il fait`.
A: {{`Il fait beau.`}}
N: `Beau` describes the weather here. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “The weather is bad” using `Il fait`.
A: {{`Il fait mauvais.`}}
N: Weather description, not a person's feeling. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “Sunshine is forecast” using the expression beginning `On annonce`.
A: {{`On annonce du soleil.`}}
N: Forecast wording from CD2 piste 27. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is raining” using the verb `pleuvoir` in the present.
A: {{`Il pleut.`}}
N: The infinitive is `pleuvoir`; future proche `il va pleuvoir`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is snowing” using the verb `neiger` in the present.
A: {{`Il neige.`}}
N: Weather description, manuel p. 104. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give the present-tense wind expression taught in the manual, using `venter`.
A: {{`Il vente.`}}
N: The manual also groups this with `C'est venteux`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Read −5 °C aloud in French, including the unit.
A: {{`Moins cinq degrés Celsius.`}}
N: Use `moins` for a temperature below zero. Manuel p. 103. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What does `la canicule` mean in the opening dialogue?
A: A heatwave, a period of very hot weather.
N: Benoit says {{`C'est la canicule !`}} in CD2 piste 26. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a weather report” using the noun `bulletin`.
A: {{`Un bulletin météo.`}}
N: One of episode 14's objectives is to understand a weather report. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What does `un bureau climatisé` mean?
A: An air-conditioned office.
N: Hélène's office is air-conditioned in the printed dialogue, CD2 piste 26. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a fan” in French, meaning the appliance in the heatwave dialogue.
A: {{`Un ventilateur.`}}
N: Hélène is going to get the fans out for the night. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What does `une journée fraiche` mean in the weather discussion?
A: A cool day.
N: `Fraiche` agrees with feminine `journée`; rectified spelling. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : je [vais|aller, présent] porter un chapeau.
N: `Aller` at the present tense introduces the infinitive. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : tu [vas|aller, présent] regarder la météo.
N: `Tu vas` + infinitive. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : elle [va|aller, présent] apporter un parapluie.
N: `Il`, `elle` and `on` share `va`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : nous [allons|aller, présent] acheter des vêtements.
N: `Nous allons` + infinitive. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : vous [allez|aller, présent] prendre le métro.
N: `Vous allez` + infinitive. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

C: Futur proche : elles [vont|aller, présent] jouer dans le sable.
N: `Ils` and `elles` share `vont`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What is the construction of the French `futur proche`?
A: Subject + `aller` in the present + the main verb in the infinitive.
N: Example: {{`Je vais sortir.`}} Manuel p. 105. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Where does `ne…pas` go in a negative `futur proche` sentence?
A: Around the conjugated form of `aller`, before the infinitive.
N: Example: {{`Je ne vais pas sortir.`}} Added grammar practice. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is going to rain” using the `futur proche`.
A: {{`Il va pleuvoir.`}}
N: Use the infinitive `pleuvoir`, not the present form `pleut`. CD2 piste 30. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “It is not going to rain” using the `futur proche`.
A: {{`Il ne va pas pleuvoir.`}}
N: The negation surrounds `va`. Added practice. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I am going to go to the beach” using the `futur proche`.
A: {{`Je vais aller à la plage.`}}
N: The first form of `aller` builds the future; the infinitive expresses movement. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What grammatical tense is `Je vais à la banque`?
A: Present indicative.
N: `Vais` is followed by a place, not an infinitive. CD2 piste 31, phrase 9. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What grammatical tense is `Je vais aller à la banque`?
A: Futur proche.
N: Present `vais` + infinitive `aller`. CD2 piste 31, phrase 8. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “tomorrow” in French.
A: {{`Demain.`}}
N: A future-time marker from the memo p. 105. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “soon” in French.
A: {{`Bientôt.`}}
N: A future-time marker; retain the circumflex. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: What does `actuellement` mean in the time-marker memo?
A: Currently, at present.
N: It does not mean English “actually”; `en fait` can express “actually” as “in fact”. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “next Monday” in French.
A: {{`Lundi prochain.`}}
N: Masculine `lundi` takes `prochain`. Manuel p. 105. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “next week” in French.
A: {{`La semaine prochaine.`}}
N: Feminine `semaine` takes `prochaine`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “next year” in French.
A: {{`L'année prochaine.`}}
N: Feminine `année` takes `prochaine`. A futur-proche project can concern next year. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a swimsuit” in French.
A: {{`Un maillot de bain.`}}
N: CD2 piste 32, item 1, manuel p. 106. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a hat” in French using the word beginning `chap-`.
A: {{`Un chapeau.`}}
N: CD2 piste 32, item 2. A winter knit hat is `une tuque` in the Québec vocabulary. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give the familiar Québec word for the flip-flops in the clothing list, with its plural article.
A: {{`Des gougounes.`}}
N: The manual labels them `sandales`. CD2 piste 32, item 3. [[fr/episode-14]]

