Q: Give the word for “a sweater” used in the Québec clothing list, with its indefinite article.
A: {{`Un chandail.`}}
N: CD2 piste 33, item 8. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a jacket” using `veste` and its indefinite article.
A: {{`Une veste.`}}
N: Feminine. CD2 piste 33, item 9. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Give the Québec word for “mittens” with its plural article.
A: {{`Des mitaines.`}}
N: CD2 piste 33, item 10. The fingers share a compartment, with a separate thumb. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “a winter coat” in French.
A: {{`Un manteau d'hiver.`}}
N: CD2 piste 33, item 11. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “trousers / pants” using the plural form in the manual's clothing list.
A: {{`Des pantalons.`}}
N: CD2 piste 33, item 12. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I am wearing a coat” using `porter` in the present.
A: {{`Je porte un manteau.`}}
N: `Porter` means to wear in the clothing context. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I feel hot” using `avoir`, about your own sensation.
A: {{`J'ai chaud.`}}
N: The weather expression is `Il fait chaud`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I feel cold” using `avoir`, about your own sensation.
A: {{`J'ai froid.`}}
N: The weather expression is `Il fait froid`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Ask “What is the weather like?” using inversion with `faire`.
A: {{`Quel temps fait-il ?`}}
N: An open question. Added practice from the weather descriptions. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Ask “What will the weather be like tomorrow?” using the futur proche and inversion.
A: {{`Quel temps va-t-il faire demain ?`}}
N: `Il va faire` becomes `va-t-il faire`. [[fr/episode-14#demander-la-météo-de-demain|Tomorrow's weather]]

---

Q: Ask “What will the weather be like tomorrow?” using `est-ce que` and the futur proche.
A: {{`Quel temps est-ce qu'il va faire demain ?`}}
N: Keep subject–verb order after `est-ce que`. [[fr/episode-14#demander-la-météo-de-demain|Tomorrow's weather]]

---

Q: Ask tomorrow's temperature using `Quelle température`, the futur proche and inversion.
A: {{`Quelle température va-t-il faire demain ?`}}
N: This asks for degrees, rather than general weather conditions. [[fr/episode-14#demander-la-météo-de-demain|Tomorrow's temperature]]

---

Q: Ask “Is it going to rain tomorrow?” using `est-ce que`.
A: {{`Est-ce qu'il va pleuvoir demain ?`}}
N: Closed yes/no question. Example negative reply: {{`Non, il ne va pas pleuvoir.`}} [[fr/episode-14#demander-la-météo-de-demain|Forecast question]]

---

Q: What is the purpose of the extra `t` in `va-t-il`?
A: It links the sounds for pronunciation between `va` and `il`; it does not change the verb's meaning.
N: Write two hyphens: `va-t-il`. `Fait-il` already has a final `t` in the verb. [[fr/episode-14#demander-la-météo-de-demain|Inversion]]

---

Q: Say “Tomorrow, I am going to ride my bike” using `faire du vélo` and the futur proche.
A: {{`Demain, je vais faire du vélo.`}}
N: Future version of the cycling answer in the class discussion. Practice sentence; no real forecast is asserted. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Change `Je consulte les bulletins météo sur Internet` to the futur proche.
A: {{`Je vais consulter les bulletins météo sur Internet.`}}
N: Present `vais` + infinitive `consulter`. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I prefer cold weather” using the present form `je` and `le froid`.
A: {{`Je préfère le froid.`}}
N: Keep the grave accent in `préfère`. Preference uses the present here. [[fr/episode-14]]

---

Q: Say “I know this word” using the present of `connaitre`.
A: {{`Je connais ce mot.`}}
N: Model answer to `Connaissez-vous le mot canicule ?` [[fr/episode-14]]
